# File contexts this profile adds to the reference policy.
#
# install/server/selinux-server.sh renders these into the policy store's
# file_contexts.local -- the same file `semanage fcontext -a` would write, in
# the same format, at the path libselinux looks for it. Writing it directly is
# what lets the `selinux` addon leave selinux-python (and setools, and 453 MiB
# of scientific Python) to the separate `selinux-tools` addon.
#
# Format: <path regex> <whitespace> <SELinux type>. Comments and blank lines
# are ignored. The setup leaf adds the user and role, and adds a level only if
# the loaded policy is MLS -- refpolicy-arch is built TYPE=standard and is not,
# so a hand-written `:s0` here would be an INVALID context, silently dropped by
# setfiles with the rule it belongs to. Write the bare type.
#
# A file context is the right tool when the policy already has rules for the
# type and only the path is wrong. Where a rule is genuinely missing, it goes
# in omarchy_server.te beside this file.
#
# /usr/share/omarchy/bin holds programs, not data, and the reference policy's
# default for anything below /usr/share is usr_t. Labelling them bin_t is what
# lets the existing policy treat them as what they are: a root shell executes
# them and systemd execs them from a unit, and both of those are questions the
# policy already has answers for. /usr/share/omarchy/install is the setup tree
# -- sourced, never executed -- and stays data.

/usr/share/omarchy/bin(/.*)?	bin_t

# /var/lib/lastlog holds shadow's lastlog2.db, which sshd writes on every
# login. refpolicy knows lastlog_t and grants sshd_t what it needs on it, but
# only names /var/log/lastlog -- shadow moved the database to /var/lib and the
# policy has not followed. Eight of the eighteen denials on a bare boot were
# this one file.
#
# Labelling the path is the whole fix, and it is the right shape of fix:
# the alternative is a policy rule letting sshd_t write every var_lib_t file on
# the system in order to reach one database.
/var/lib/lastlog(/.*)?	lastlog_t
